We wanted to share some of the concepts we've been working on because we have made a noticeable difference in consistency and shot quality.
🏓 Focus on consistency. The players who improve the fastest are often the ones who make fewer unforced errors and execute the basics well.
Here are 7 game-changing habits that we used to improve our consistency:
🧠 1. Keep Your Paddle in Your Field of Vision
For drops, resets, and dinks, keep your backswing compact. If your paddle disappears behind your body, consistency disappears with it.
👁️ 2. Track the Ball with Your Head
Don't lift your head early to see where the ball is going. Follow the ball all the way to contact and focus on hitting the center of the paddle face. Nor applicable when doing fast counter attacks.
👟 3. Get Your Feet Set Before Contact
Trying to hit while still moving often leads to poor touch and missed shots. Plant your feet and establish balance before your opponent strikes the ball.
🛠️ 4. Prioritize Control Before Power
Power paddles are popular, but touch, resets, drops, and dinks win more rallies. Master control first—power can come later.
🛡️ 5. Maintain a High Ready Position
Keep your paddle up and out in front. This reduces reaction time, improves hand battles, and helps you stay engaged during long rallies.
📐 6. Master Paddle Face & Grip Pressure
Your paddle face determines where the ball goes. Too much grip pressure can drive balls into the net; too little can create pop-ups. Learn to adjust and stay aware.
The ball normally goes where your paddle face is pointing at contact. Be hyper-aware of your grip pressure: squeezing too hard closes the face (sending the ball into the net), while a super loose grip opens it up (creating easy pop-ups for your opponents).
Pro tip: Lock your wrist to keep the face stable on backhand dinks, but loosen it slightly on forehand dinks to help angle the face down and toward your target.
🏓 7. Choke Up for Faster Hands
At the kitchen line, moving your hand slightly up the handle can improve paddle control and increase hand speed during quick exchanges.
💡 Remember: Consistency is not about making spectacular shots—it's about making the right shot, over and over again.
The biggest lesson from last week? Improvement doesn't always come from playing more games. Sometimes it comes from playing fewer games and spending more time drilling with purpose in a smaller group setting.
